<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=book-of-optics">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Optics</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1011">1011</a>–<a href="/years/1021">1021</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Kitāb al-Manāẓir, Kitab al-Manazir, De Aspectibus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Optics is a seven-volume treatise on optics written between 1011 and 1021 by the medieval Arab scholar Ibn al-Haytham, known in the West as Alhazen (965–c. 1040). It argues on experimental grounds against the extramission theory of vision held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euclid">Euclid</a> and Ptolemy, and proposes that sight takes place by light entering the eye, the model accepted today. It also describes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camera-obscura">camera obscura</a>. Translated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> around 1200 and printed by Friedrich Risner in 1572, it strongly influenced European optics, physics and mathematics between the 13th and 17th centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-optics.jpg" alt="Book of Optics" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown illustrator · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thesaurus_opticus_Titelblatt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Khwarizmi’s book on algebra, written in Baghdad around 820, gave the subject its name; Ibn al-Haytham’s <b>Book of Optics</b> transformed the study of light; and the Canon of Medicine of Ibn Sina and the commentaries of Ibn Rushd on Aristotle, translated into Latin, shaped European learning for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/language/arabic">Arabic · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Ibn al-Haytham’s <b>Book of Optics</b>, written in Cairo in the early 11th century, shaped European optics into the 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Background</a></li></ul></section>
